Questions:
Title: Why is printing &quot;B&quot; dramatically slower than printing &quot;#&quot;?.
Body: <p>I generated two matrices of <code>1000</code> x <code>1000</code>:</p>
<p>First Matrix: <code>O</code> and <code>#</code>.<br />
Second Matrix: <code>O</code> and <code>B</code>.</p>
<p>Using the following code, the first matrix took 8.52 seconds to complete:</p>
<pre><code>Random r = new Random();
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; 1000; j++) {
        if (r.nextInt(4) == 0) {
            System.out.print(&quot;O&quot;);
        } else {
            System.out.print(&quot;#&quot;);
        }
    }

   System.out.println(&quot;&quot;);
 }
</code></pre>
<p>With this code, the second matrix took 259.152 seconds to complete:</p>
<pre><code>Random r = new Random();
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; 1000; j++) {
        if (r.nextInt(4) == 0) {
            System.out.print(&quot;O&quot;);
        } else {
            System.out.print(&quot;B&quot;); // only line changed
        }
    }

    System.out.println(&quot;&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>What is the reason behind the dramatically different run times?</p>
<hr />
<p>As suggested in the comments, printing only <code>System.out.print(&quot;#&quot;);</code> takes <code>7.8871</code> seconds, whereas <code>System.out.print(&quot;B&quot;);</code> gives <code>still printing...</code>.</p>
<p>As others pointed out that it works for them normally, I tried <a href="http://ideone.com" rel="noreferrer">Ideone.com</a> for instance, and both pieces of code execute at the same speed.</p>
<p>Test Conditions:</p>
<ul>
<li>I ran this test from <em>Netbeans 7.2</em>, with the output into its console</li>
<li>I used <code>System.nanoTime()</code> for measurements</li>
</ul>

Answers:
```python
import random
import time

# Function to generate a matrix of O and #
def generate_matrix(size):
    matrix = []
    for _ in range(size):
        row = []
        for _ in range(size):
            if random.randint(0, 3) == 0:
                row.append('O')
            else:
                row.append('#')
        matrix.append(''.join(row))
    return '\n'.join(matrix)

# Function to generate a matrix of O and B
def generate_matrix_b(size):
    matrix = []
    for _ in range(size):
        row = []
        for _ in range(size):
            if random.randint(0, 3) == 0:
                row.append('O')
            else:
                row.append('B')
        matrix.append(''.join(row))
    return '\n'.join(matrix)

# Test the functions
size = 1000
matrix_o = generate_matrix(size)
matrix_b = generate_matrix_b(size)

# Measure the time taken to print the matrices
start_time = time.time()
print(matrix_o)
print(matrix_b)
end_time = time.time()

print(f"Time taken to print matrix O: {end_time - start_time} seconds")
print(f"Time taken to print matrix B: {end_time - start_time} seconds")
```

The reason behind the dramatically different run times is due to the way Java handles Unicode characters. The character 'B' is a Unicode character that requires more processing power to print than the character '#'. In Java, when you print a character using `System.out.print()`, it first checks if the character is a Unicode character. If it is, it then calls the `unicodeCharAt()` method to get the Unicode code point of the character, which can be a time-consuming operation. This is why printing 'B' is slower than printing '#'.

