def max_pool_with_same_padding(input, kernel_size):
    return tf.nn.max_pool(input, [1, kernel_size, kernel_size, 1], [1, kernel_size, kernel_size, 1], 'SAME')

def max_pool_with_valid_padding(input, kernel_size):
    return tf.nn.max_pool(input, [1, kernel_size, kernel_size, 1], [1, kernel_size, kernel_size, 1], 'VALID')assistant

The difference between 'SAME' and 'VALID' padding in tf.nn.max_pool of tensorflow is as follows:

- 'SAME' padding means that the output will have the same spatial dimensions as the input. This is achieved by adding padding to the input so that the spatial dimensions remain the same after max pooling. The number of padding is calculated as (kernel_size - 1) // 2.

- 'VALID' padding, on the other hand, means that there will be no zero padding outside the edges when we do max pool. This is achieved by not adding any padding to the input. The output spatial dimensions will be (input spatial dimension - kernel_size + 1).

In summary, 'SAME' padding maintains the spatial dimensions of the input, while 'VALID' padding does not add any padding and thus reduces the spatial dimensions of the input.

