Title: Can we instantiate an abstract class?.
Body: <p>During one of my interview, I was asked "If we can instantiate an abstract class?"</p>

<p>My reply was "No. we can't". But, interviewer told me "Wrong, we can." </p>

<p>I argued a bit on this. Then he told me to try this myself at home. </p>

<pre><code>abstract class my {
    public void mymethod() {
        System.out.print("Abstract");
    }
}

class poly {
    public static void main(String a[]) {
        my m = new my() {};
        m.mymethod();
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Here, I'm creating instance of my class and calling method of abstract class. Can anyone please explain this to me? Was I really wrong during my interview?</p>

Answers:
```python
# In Python, abstract classes are not directly supported like in Java or C++.
# However, we can create a metaclass that enforces the use of abstract methods.

import abc

# Define an abstract class with an abstract method
class MyClass(metaclass=abc.ABCMeta):
    @abc.abstractmethod
    def mymethod(self):
        pass

# Try to instantiate the abstract class
try:
    obj = MyClass()
except TypeError as e:
    print(e)  # Output: Can't instantiate abstract class MyClass with abstract methods mymethod

# Define a concrete class that inherits from the abstract class
class MyConcreteClass(MyClass):
    def mymethod(self):
        print("Concrete")

# Instantiate the concrete class and call the abstract method
obj = MyConcreteClass()
obj.mymethod()  # Output: Concrete
```

