    String str4 = "1927-12-31 23:54:08";
    Date sDt3 = sf.parse(str3);
    Date sDt4 = sf.parse(str4);
    long ld3 = sDt3.getTime() / 1000;
    long ld4 = sDt4.getTime() / 1000;
    System.out.println(ld4 - ld3);
}
```

# Explanation

The issue arises due to the timezone offset. The `SimpleDateFormat` class uses the default timezone, which is `Asia/Shanghai` in this case, with an offset of 28800000 seconds (or 8 hours) from UTC. This means that when the program calculates the time difference between `sDt3` and `sDt4`, it is actually comparing times 8 hours apart, not just 1 second.

To fix this issue, you should use the `Calendar` class to account for the timezone offset:

```java
Calendar cal = Calendar.getInstance();
cal.setTimeZone(TimeZone.getTimeZone("GMT"));
cal.setTime(sDt3);
long ld3 = cal.getTimeInMillis() / 1000;

cal.setTime(sDt4);
long ld4 = cal.getTimeInMillis() / 1000;
System.out.println(ld4 - ld3);
```

Alternatively, you can use the `java.time` package, which is more accurate and easier to use:

```java
ZonedDateTime zdt1 = ZonedDateTime.parse("1927-12-31 23:54:07", DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss"));
ZonedDateTime zdt2 = ZonedDateTime.parse("1927-12-31 23:54:08", DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss"));
long ld3 = zdt2.toInstant().toEpochMilli();
long ld4 = zdt1.toInstant().toEpochMilli();
System.out.println(ld4 - ld3);
```

Note that the `java.time` package is available in Java 8 and later versions.

