Title: Iterate through object properties.
Body: <p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var obj = {
    name: "Simon",
    age: "20",
    clothing: {
        style: "simple",
        hipster: false
    }
}

for(var propt in obj){
    console.log(propt + ': ' + obj[propt]);
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

<p>How does the variable <code>propt</code> represent the properties of the object? It's not a built-in method or property. Why does it come up with every property in the object?</p>

Answers:
```python
# Define a dictionary
obj = {
    "name": "Simon",
    "age": "20",
    "clothing": {
        "style": "simple",
        "hipster": False
    }
}

# Iterate through the object properties
for prop in obj:
    # Check if the property is a key in the object (not in its nested objects)
    if isinstance(obj[prop], dict):
        print(f"Property: {prop}, Value: {obj[prop]}")
    else:
        print(f"Property: {prop}, Value: {obj[prop]}")
```

The variable `prop` represents the keys (or properties) of the object. In the context of the provided JavaScript code, `for(var propt in obj)` iterates over the object's keys, which include the top-level keys and the keys of the nested object. However, the JavaScript code only logs the top-level keys, not the nested keys. The reason for this is due to the way the `for...in` loop works in JavaScript. It iterates over the object's own enumerable properties, including inherited properties. To avoid logging the inherited properties, the JavaScript code only checks if the property is a key in the object itself, not in its nested objects. 

In the provided Python code, we use `isinstance(obj[prop], dict)` to check if the property value is a dictionary (i.e., a nested object). If it's not a dictionary, we simply print the property and its value.

