Title: Why use getters and setters/accessors?.
Body: <p>What's the advantage of using getters and setters - that only get and set - instead of simply using public fields for those variables?</p>
<p>If getters and setters are ever doing more than just the simple get/set, I can figure this one out very quickly, but I'm not 100% clear on how:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public String foo;
</code></pre>
<p>is any worse than:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>private String foo;
public void setFoo(String foo) { this.foo = foo; }
public String getFoo() { return foo; }
</code></pre>
<p>Whereas the former takes a lot less boilerplate code.</p>

Answers:
```python
def get_foo(self):
    return self._foo

def set_foo(self, foo):
    if not isinstance(foo, str):
        raise TypeError("foo must be a string")
    self._foo = foo
```

```python
# Single and double leading underscores are used for name mangling in Python.
# Name mangling is a mechanism to make class names or instance names harder to access directly.

class Test:
    def __init__(self):
        self.__single_leading_underscore = "Single leading underscore"
        self.__double_leading_underscore = "Double leading underscore"

obj = Test()
print(obj.__single_leading_underscore)  # Output: Single leading underscore
print(obj.__double_leading_underscore)  # Output: Double leading underscore

try:
    print(obj._Test__single_leading_underscore)  # Raises AttributeError
except AttributeError:
    print("Double leading underscore is protected, not private.")

try:
    print(obj._Test__double_leading_underscore)  # Raises AttributeError
except AttributeError:
    print("Double leading underscore is protected, not private.")
```

In the above code, we are trying to access the private variables of the class `Test`. The single leading underscore makes the variable protected, while the double leading underscore makes it private. However, Python does not provide a strict enforcement of privacy, and these variables can still be accessed using their mangled names.

from typing import TypeVar, Generic

T = TypeVar('T')

class FootballTeam(Generic[T]):
    def __init__(self, team_name: str, running_total: int):
        self.team_name = team_name
        self.running_total = running_total
        self.players: List[T] = []

    def add_player(self, player: T) -> None:
        self.players.append(player)

    def count(self) -> int:
        return len(self.players)

    def __len__(self) -> int:
        return self.count()

    def __getitem__(self, index: int) -> T:
        return self.players[index]

    def __setitem__(self, index: int, value: T) -> None:
        self.players[index] = value

    def __delitem__(self, index: int) -> None:
        del self.players[index]

    def __iter__(self) -> Iterator[T]:
        return iter(self.players)

    def __contains__(self, item: T) -> bool:
        return item in self.players

    def __str__(self) -> str:
        return f"FootballTeam(name='{self.team_name}', running_total={self.running_total})"

    def __repr__(self) -> str:
        return f"FootballTeam(name='{self.team_name}', running_total={self.running_total})"

